{"hash":"cbed5cd1af49a8d3","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":85.002,"said":"From back here you can take in the whole tower: plain, massive stone under whitewash, seventy-two feet high, its rounded courses closing toward the top like a lotus bud. On its summit sit three golden kalashas, given, the district records say, by the Maharaja of Gidhaur, and above them stands not a trident but a Panchshool, a five-pointed one, which is rare. Before Mahashivratri the Panchshools are taken down for worship, and then raised again for the festival. And who built all this? No one knows for certain. The oldest name the records give is Puran Mal, an ancestor of those same Gidhaur Maharajas, who is said to have built parts of the front of the temple in 1596, in the reign of the Emperor Akbar. Nearly two centuries later came a very different visitor. In 1788 Mr Keating, the East India Company’s collector at Birbhum, sent his assistant to take charge of the temple’s offerings. But when Keating came to Deoghar himself, he gave up interfering, and left the temple in the hands of its high priest. And so the worship went on, as it always had. Now come round to the porch, where Baba’s day begins before dawn.\n\nDid you know? Inside the top of the tower is an eight-petalled lotus jewel called the Chandrakanta Mani.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1127},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1143,"end":1233}],"words":[[0.098,0.228,0,4],[0.338,0.3,5,9],[0.651,0.252,10,14],[0.915,0.108,15,18],[1.036,0.168,19,22],[1.216,0.228,23,27],[1.456,0.084,28,30],[1.552,0.084,31,34],[1.649,0.264,35,40],[1.925,0.505,41,46],[2.622,0.445,48,53],[3.283,0.433,55,62],[3.728,0.469,63,68],[4.209,0.264,69,74],[4.485,0.637,75,84],[5.314,0.613,86,97],[5.939,0.3,98,102],[6.252,0.361,103,107],[6.829,0.204,109,112],[7.045,0.433,113,120],[7.49,0.457,121,128],[7.959,0.481,129,136],[8.451,0.312,137,143],[8.776,0.108,144,147],[8.896,0.312,148,151],[9.221,0.228,152,156],[9.461,0.036,157,158],[9.509,0